#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124323 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "c2696e2e-7aa9-5831-9028-9adfe5889bbe" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating the Derivative of a Cartesian Function With Respect to Plane Polar Coordinates"}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "4940fa22-1584-565f-81fe-dc12e5132f23" :question/math-academy-id "e-8294" :question/problem "Given that $f(x,y)=\\dfrac12x^2+y^2$, find $\\dfrac{\\partial f}{\\partial \\theta}$, where $(x,y)$ are Cartesian coordinates and $(r,\\theta)$ are the corresponding plane polar coordinates." :question/requires-calculator false :question/worked-solution "Applying the multivariable chain rule for the function $f(x,y)$ with two independent variables $r$ and $\\theta$, where\n\n$$\nx=r\\cos\\theta\n$$\n\nand\n\n$$\ny=r\\sin\\theta\n$$\n\nwe have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\frac{\\partial f}{\\partial \\theta } &= \\frac{\\partial f}{\\partial x} \\cdot \\frac{\\partial x}{\\partial \\theta } + \\frac{\\partial f}{\\partial y} \\cdot \\frac{\\partial y}{\\partial \\theta } \\\\\n&=-\\frac{\\partial f}{\\partial x}r\\sin \\theta  + \\frac{\\partial f}{\\partial y}r\\cos \\theta \n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFirst, we find the partial derivatives of $f$ with respect to $x$ and $y{:}$\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\partial f}{\\partial x} = x, \\frac{\\partial f}{\\partial y} = 2y\n$$\n\nWriting the above results in terms of $r$ and $\\theta$, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\frac{\\partial f}{\\partial x} &= r\\cos \\theta , \\\\\n\\frac{\\partial f}{\\partial y} &= 2r\\sin \\theta \n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, substituting these into our chain rule formula, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\frac{\\partial f}{\\partial \\theta } &= - (r\\cos \\theta )r\\sin \\theta  + (2r\\sin \\theta )r\\cos \\theta  \\\\\n&=-r^{2}\\sin \\theta \\cos \\theta  + 2r^{2}\\sin \\theta \\cos \\theta  \\\\\n&= r^{2}\\sin \\theta \\cos \\theta  \\\\\n&= \\frac{1}{2}r^{2}\\sin (2\\theta )\n\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124324 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "879dfc0f-ea2b-5ccb-bbc6-95f43b9c7073" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating the Derivative of a Polar Function With Respect to Cartesian Coordinates"}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "a0246ee6-702f-5d8d-8482-8db1c46b32ed" :question/math-academy-id "e-8295" :question/problem "Given that $w(r, \\theta)=r \\cos 3\\theta$, find $\\dfrac{\\partial w}{\\partial x}$, where $(x,y)$ are Cartesian coordinates and $(r,\\theta)$ are the corresponding plane polar coordinates." :question/requires-calculator false :question/worked-solution "We will make use of the following chain rule formula:\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\partial w}{\\partial x} = \\dfrac{\\partial w}{\\partial r}\\cos\\theta - \\dfrac{1}{r}\\dfrac{\\partial w}{\\partial \\theta}\\sin\\theta\n$$\n\nFirst, we find the partial derivatives of $w$ with respect to $r$ and $\\theta{:}$\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\partial w}{\\partial r} = \\cos 3\\theta , \\frac{\\partial w}{\\partial \\theta } =-3r\\sin 3\\theta \n$$\n\nThen, substituting these into our chain rule formula, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\frac{\\partial w}{\\partial x} &= (\\cos 3\\theta )\\cos \\theta  - \\frac{1}{r}(-3r\\sin 3\\theta )\\sin \\theta  \\\\\n&= \\cos \\theta \\cos 3\\theta  + 3\\sin \\theta \\sin 3\\theta \n\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124325 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "2ee45610-e14c-593e-9002-e2b2fcdb4517" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating the Derivative of a Cartesian Function With Respect to Cylindrical Polar Coordinates"}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "c1e69bbe-b079-56e5-9ce0-296785d73100" :question/math-academy-id "e-8296" :question/problem "Given that $w(x,y,z)=z^3(x^2+2y^2)$, find $\\dfrac{\\partial w}{\\partial \\theta}$, where $(x,y,z)$ are Cartesian coordinates and $(r,\\theta,z)$ are the corresponding cylindrical polar coordinates." :question/requires-calculator false :question/worked-solution "Applying the multivariable chain rule for the function $w(x,y,z)$ with three independent variables $r, \\theta$, and $z$, where\n\n$$\nx=r\\cos\\theta, y=r\\sin\\theta\n$$\n\nand $z=z$, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\frac{\\partial w}{\\partial \\theta } &= \\frac{\\partial w}{\\partial x} \\cdot \\frac{\\partial x}{\\partial \\theta } + \\frac{\\partial w}{\\partial y} \\cdot \\frac{\\partial y}{\\partial \\theta } + \\frac{\\partial w}{\\partial z} \\cdot \\frac{\\partial z}{\\partial \\theta } \\\\\n&=-\\frac{\\partial w}{\\partial x}r\\sin \\theta  + \\frac{\\partial w}{\\partial y}r\\cos \\theta \n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFirst, we find the partial derivatives of $w$ with respect to $x$ and $y{:}$\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\partial w}{\\partial x} = 2xz^{3}, \\frac{\\partial w}{\\partial y} = 4yz^{3}\n$$\n\nWriting the above results in terms of $r, \\theta$, and $z$, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\frac{\\partial w}{\\partial x} &= 2rz^{3}\\cos \\theta , \\\\\n\\frac{\\partial w}{\\partial y} &= 4rz^{3}\\sin \\theta \n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, substituting these into our chain rule formula, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\frac{\\partial w}{\\partial \\theta } &= - (2rz^{3}\\cos \\theta )r\\sin \\theta  + (4rz^{3}\\sin \\theta )r\\cos \\theta  \\\\\n&=-2r^{2}z^{3}\\cos \\theta \\sin \\theta  + 4r^{2}z^{3}\\sin \\theta \\cos \\theta  \\\\\n&= 2r^{2}z^{3}\\sin \\theta \\cos \\theta  \\\\\n&= r^{2}z^{3}\\sin 2\\theta \n\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186125159 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "2ee45610-e14c-593e-9002-e2b2fcdb4517" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating the Derivative of a Cartesian Function With Respect to Cylindrical Polar Coordinates"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "9d03f6b2-1bf3-5896-b6db-efa0d4b1f9bd" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2rz\\sin^{2} \\theta " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186123977} {:answer/id #uuid "390b612c-c68a-575e-a80f-974a20f1431d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2rz\\cos \\theta  + r^{2}\\sin \\theta " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186123978} {:answer/id #uuid "3e4cb3c9-268e-56d6-9db2-a8ab8e396364" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "r^{2}\\sin 2\\theta  - r\\cos \\theta " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186123979} {:answer/id #uuid "5c3b9a2f-8b5f-5200-9cf6-c9e6d4e425b8" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "r^{2}\\sin \\theta " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186123980} {:answer/id #uuid "7144ba48-e3c0-53eb-83f0-1f3ab6763675" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2rz\\cos \\theta " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186123981}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "7144ba48-e3c0-53eb-83f0-1f3ab6763675" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2rz\\cos \\theta " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186123981} :answer-field/id #uuid "166da9fd-7349-5b3a-bb26-b20f451ef463" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186123976}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "6edb4185-988d-5465-a73c-d87078eb5744" :question/math-academy-id "q-94770" :question/problem "Given that $f(x, y, z) = x^{2} + y^{2} + 2yz$, find $\\frac{\\partial f}{\\partial \\theta }$, where $(x, y, z)$ are Cartesian coordinates and $(r, \\theta , z)$ are the corresponding cylindrical polar coordinates." :question/requires-calculator false :question/worked-solution "In cylindrical coordinates, $x^2+y^2=r^2$ and $y=r\\sin\\theta$, so $$f=r^2+2rz\\sin\\theta.$$ Hold $r$ and $z$ fixed while differentiating with respect to $\\theta$: $$f_\\theta=2rz\\cos\\theta.$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186125164 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "2ee45610-e14c-593e-9002-e2b2fcdb4517" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating the Derivative of a Cartesian Function With Respect to Cylindrical Polar Coordinates"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "8de20450-8e75-5421-a8fd-9f931881b4dd" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "r^{2}\\sin^{2} \\theta  + 2rz\\cos \\theta " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186124007} {:answer/id #uuid "b8cb917e-c20f-5a58-879d-027b5d61c72b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "r\\sin \\theta  + z^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186124008} {:answer/id #uuid "bdfabaff-d294-5863-9a4e-b1d9a9ccc3f6" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "r\\cos \\theta  + 2rz\\sin \\theta " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186124009} {:answer/id #uuid "ff28c095-8df6-523b-862d-4d69a164be9b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "r^{2}\\sin (2\\theta ) + rz^{2}\\cos \\theta " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186124010} {:answer/id #uuid "441edfd6-d4c5-5918-88d6-0b2390d3070d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "r^{2}\\sin \\theta  + 2r\\cos \\theta " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186124011}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "8de20450-8e75-5421-a8fd-9f931881b4dd" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "r^{2}\\sin^{2} \\theta  + 2rz\\cos \\theta " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186124007} :answer-field/id #uuid "ec54e909-ce31-5cb3-bf5b-33d69276af29" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186124006}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "08bad6ab-1a42-5f7a-8850-36801983c246" :question/math-academy-id "q-95047" :question/problem "Given that $f(x, y, z) = xy + y^{2}z + xz^{2}$, find $\\frac{\\partial f}{\\partial z}$, where $(x, y, z)$ are Cartesian coordinates and $(r, \\theta , z)$ are the corresponding cylindrical polar coordinates." :question/requires-calculator false :question/worked-solution "Substitution gives $$f=r^2\\cos\\theta\\sin\\theta+r^2\\sin^2\\theta\\,z+r\\cos\\theta\\,z^2.$$ Holding $r$ and $\\theta$ fixed, differentiate each term with respect to $z$ to get $$f_z=r^2\\sin^2\\theta+2rz\\cos\\theta.$$"}]}
